  "result": "I graded quiz 0001 and wrote the grades to `grades/0001-s1.json`. You got one of three right.\n\n| Item | Verdict | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| q1 | wrong (M2) | You chose \"the time one instruction takes by itself\". Pipelining doesn't shorten one instruction. It raises throughput, meaning more instructions finish in a given time. The right answer is b. |\n| q2 | wrong | You answered 100, but the answer is 103. The first instruction needs all 4 cycles to get through the pipeline. Each of the other 99 then finishes one cycle after the one before: 4 + 99 = 103. |\n| q3 | correct | \"Several instructions are being done at the same time\" is the right idea. It also contradicts your q1 answer, so it's worth a second look. |\n\nYou understand the overlap mechanism. The two things to fix are throughput versus the time of one instruction, and counting the fill time (the cycles the first instruction needs) before adding one cycle per remaining instruction.\n\nI also added `learning-records/0002-pipelining-throughput-vs-latency.md` and updated `NOTES.md`.\n\n**Next step:** redo q2 with a 5-stage pipeline and 20 instructions. Write down the fill time first, then add one cycle for each remaining instruction. This counting is the base for working out what a stall costs.",
